<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    Local network access.

    Since macOS 15 an app must declare why it talks to the local network, and
    from 26 the enforcement is thorough: without this key a GUI-launched app is
    denied LAN traffic SILENTLY. No error, no prompt — sockets simply never
    reach anything. Every symptom looks like the switcher being absent.

    It does not show up in development because a process started from Terminal
    inherits Terminal's own permission. The app only fails once it is
    double-clicked, which is to say only for the people using it.

    This app is nothing but local network traffic: the ATEM protocol on UDP
    9910, the Videohub protocol on TCP 9990, and a sweep of the local /24s to
    find them.
  -->
  <key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
  <string>BlackMatrix finds and controls Blackmagic switchers and routers on your local network.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
